@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "YAML vs Classic Pipelines";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="lead text-center">Understanding the key differences between YAML and Classic Pipelines in Azure DevOps.</p>
    <hr />

    <!-- Introduction -->
    <div class="mt-4">
        <h2 class="text-success">Introduction</h2>
        <p>
            Azure DevOps provides two primary ways to define and manage CI/CD pipelines: YAML and Classic Pipelines.
            Understanding the differences helps teams choose the right approach for their development workflows.
        </p>
        <div class="text-center my-3">
            <img src="https://example.com/yaml-vs-classic.jpg" alt="YAML vs Classic Pipelines" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg w-100">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Comparison of YAML and Classic Pipelines in Azure DevOps.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- YAML Pipelines -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-warning">YAML Pipelines</h2>
        <p>
            YAML pipelines are code-based pipelines defined using a YAML file. These pipelines enable version control,
            allow collaboration, and support automation within repositories.
        </p>
        <h3>Advantages:</h3>
        <ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
            <li class="list-group-item">Stored in version control, ensuring easy tracking and history.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">Enables Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) approach.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">Supports multi-stage pipelines and better modularization.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">Easier to maintain and reuse across projects.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Example:</h3>
        <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded">
trigger:
  - main

pool:
  vmImage: 'ubuntu-latest'

steps:
  - task: DotNetCoreCLI@2
    inputs:
      command: 'build'
      projects: '**/*.csproj'
        </pre>
    </div>

    <!-- Classic Pipelines -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-danger">Classic Pipelines</h2>
        <p>
            Classic Pipelines use a visual designer to configure CI/CD processes through a graphical interface.
            This approach is suitable for users who prefer a UI-driven experience.
        </p>
        <h3>Advantages:</h3>
        <ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
            <li class="list-group-item">No need for YAML knowledge, making it user-friendly.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">Supports drag-and-drop configurations.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item">Provides quick setup without coding.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="text-center my-3">
            <img src="https://example.com/classic-pipelines.jpg" alt="Classic Pipelines UI" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg w-100">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Classic Pipelines use a visual designer for setup.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Key Differences Table -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-info">Key Differences</h2>
        <table class="table table-bordered">
            <thead class="table-dark">
                <tr>
                    <th>Feature</th>
                    <th>YAML Pipelines</th>
                    <th>Classic Pipelines</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Definition Format</td>
                    <td>Code-based (YAML)</td>
                    <td>UI-based (Graphical Editor)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Version Control</td>
                    <td>Stored in repository, versioned</td>
                    <td>Stored in Azure DevOps, not versioned</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Flexibility</td>
                    <td>Highly flexible with scripting</td>
                    <td>Limited flexibility, predefined tasks</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Best Suited For</td>
                    <td>Developers, DevOps teams, automation-driven workflows</td>
                    <td>Users who prefer UI-based configuration</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Conclusion -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2 class="text-secondary">Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
            YAML and Classic Pipelines each serve different purposes. YAML is ideal for teams that want version control,
            automation, and flexibility, while Classic Pipelines provide a more visual and UI-driven approach.
        </p>
        <div class="text-center my-3">
            <img src="https://example.com/yaml-classic-decision.jpg" alt="Choosing the Right Pipeline" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg w-100">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Choose YAML for automation and flexibility; Classic for simplicity and ease of use.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>